this is a great topic that came up  yesterday during a live stream and it  touches on politics it touches on  finance it touches on economics so let's  let's dive right into it why did the  stock market go way up last Thursday  when the news was breaking 6.6 million  more people have lost their jobs and  have filed for unemployment benefits  because one would imagine that bad  economic news would cause the stock  market to go down not up precipitously  but the opposite happened indeed last  Thursday when the terrible unemployment  news broke stocks went absolutely  skyrocketing for the day and it has  confused a lot of different people and  it's a great opportunity to talk through  many of the different elements that that  are factors here so there's a few  different things going on first of all  if you want you can just write all of  this off as mere speculation by market  insiders right you can argue the stock  market doesn't even really have any  connection to the news of what's going  on within the economy  it's just traders speculating and  pushing stocks up and pushing stocks  down sometimes taking long positions  sometimes taking short positions when it  benefits them and there's just no  meaning whatsoever in market moves  related to what's happening in the  economy now of course there's an element  of truth to that there's no doubt that  hedge funds derivative instruments a lot  of these non-productive financial  mechanisms can benefit from and even  move markets or exacerbate market moves  without being directly tied to some  factual change on a Main Street for the  average person but it's only a piece of  it it's not really a comprehensive  analysis and it lacks some understanding  of some pretty basic economic concepts  now before I jump into this in more  detail there's two things that I should  mention number one any effort to explain  why stocks went up or down on a  particular day is a fool's errand in the  sense that there's no real way to  definitively say here's why the market  was up or here's why the market was down  on any particular day we're doing this  more of as a thought experiment  number two I understand that lots of the  ideas that I'm gonna use today are  partially debunked in the sense that  standard economic theory a rational  actor efficient market economics has  been partially debunked and that is  undeniable but it still is an okay fret  framework as an entry point to start  thinking about some of these things so  all of those disclaimers out of the way  let's now play the game so the first  thing to understand is that the stock  market is supposed to already reflect  future news that we can expect this is a  classic economics term called efficient  markets hypothesis and put very simply  what what efficient markets hypothesis  asserts is that asset prices the price  of the Dow the Nasdaq the S&amp;P 500 it  reflects all available information now  sometimes information is available but  it's about a future event that hasn't  happened yet efficient markets  hypothesis argues information about  future events is also priced into the  stock market  now of course efficient market  hypothesis is one of those things they  teach us early in economics academia and  to some degree it is complete and total  BS it's only sort of true if the markets  really priced in all future information  there wouldn't be any way to make money  in the market and you know eventually  brought to a absurdity eventually the  Sun will expand and engulf the earth and  everybody on the planet will die unless  we move to some other planet if that was  priced in stock markets would be at zero  because we already know that that's  eventually going to happen so again it's  just an idea it's a framework it doesn't  work 100% but as applied to the  unemployment numbers from last week even  though the 6.6 million additional people  filing for unemployment was announced on  Thursday it was expected for more than a  week ahead of time and there were  predictions about the number of millions  of people that would be filing for  unemployment so arguably it was already  priced into the market now that could  explain why stocks didn't crash on the  announcement of unemployment numbers  last Thursday but it doesn't really  explain why stocks actually went up  drastically on that news that doesn't  seem to make sense well that gets us to  layer two of the analyses which is  markets don't like uncertainty even  though there was the expectation of  millions of people losing their jobs but  last week once it was announced the  uncertainty was removed so if you expect  six point six million people to lose  their jobs and then it's confirmed that  six point six million people lose their  jobs you've removed the uncertainty six  point six million plus the uncertainty  of will it be more will it be less is  worse arguably then just knowing it's  six point six million people who lost  their jobs so that could make stocks go  up lastly when you file for unemployment  benefits you start to get money so from  a market perspective what is going to  happen to the companies on the stock  market when you learn almost 7 million  people are going to start getting money  they're going to start getting  unemployment benefits coming in which  they will spend with a lot of these  companies that trade on the stock market  that could cause stocks to go up on the  news of 6.6 million new unemployment  claims and then lastly when you learn  that nearly 7 million more people lost  their jobs meaning around 17 million  people have lost jobs in just three  weeks that's a signal that this is bad  enough that the government might do more  stimulus and again stimulus injects  money much of which goes to the  companies that are traded on various  stock exchanges so thinking about it  this way we at least have a plausible  explanation for why terrible  unemployment news correlates with the  stock market going up lastly remember  this is more proof that the stock market  going up doesn't mean that Main Street  or the average worker individually is  doing well six trillion and stimulus  could be great in the aggregate for the  market but if the individual person is  getting a $1,200 check  seven weeks after they've been sitting  at home or whatever the case may be it  may be more like five weeks in some  cases you can very quickly see how  aggregate market stimulus can be very  good for the stock market but not that  good for the individual person who has  way more than twelve hundred dollars in  bills overdue and is just now getting  their $1200 check let me know if this  type of segment is useful or interesting  and you know I try to explain this is  the economic concept it's partially BS  right I mean a lot of standard economics  is partially BS but it can still serve  to be useful in thinking about a  particular news event in this case bad  unemployment news combined with the  stock market  going up
 

